expeditions/2026-06-20-aoyagi-full/threads/36-genM-jacobian-det/codex/achiever-path-answer.md:110:The only caveat is non-uniqueness. For `M=(2,2,1)`, the two minimizers give different residual-block distributions: `1+1` across two layers or `0+2` in the final layer. So there is no unique `M`-only active set unless the achiever/minimizer chart is chosen first. Once `T*` is chosen, the rule is uniform.
expeditions/2026-06-20-aoyagi-full/threads/genm-sjjoint-design/codex/corank-answer.md:5:Aoyagi’s coupled `diag(b)` recursion is a sequential explicit chart construction. It is not a call to abstract Hironaka, and Case 1’s shared-divisor merge is not a simultaneous principalisation. The formal work is large, but it is bounded chart algebra plus a divisor-support ledger.
expeditions/2026-06-20-aoyagi-full/threads/genm-sjjoint-design/codex/corank-answer.md:7:The caveat: this is true for the **native Aoyagi `(S,J)` blow-up route**. If one first integrates out a block and creates a Gram determinant such as `det(Q_b Q_bᵀ)` over a degenerate matrix product, then completing that atom route really does become a determinantal/multi-ideal principalisation problem. That is Claim-B’s real warning, but it is about the wrong route for this question.
expeditions/2026-06-20-aoyagi-full/threads/genm-sjjoint-design/codex/corank-answer.md:18:Under that interpretation, Claim-A and the coupled `diag(b)` construction are the same object.
expeditions/2026-06-20-aoyagi-full/threads/genm-sjjoint-design/codex/corank-answer.md:20:But coupled `diag(b)` does **more** than a fresh-per-block or per-row iteration. A fresh model that gives new independent variables to each row/block forgets sharing. That is exactly why threshold-only/per-row models fail: they cannot distinguish `⟨δx,δy⟩` from `⟨δ₁x,δ₂y⟩`.
expeditions/2026-06-20-aoyagi-full/threads/genm-sjjoint-design/codex/corank-answer.md:60:The shared support is necessary, but it is recorded by the sequential `diag(b)` recursion. It does not force a simultaneous principalisation unless you choose the Gram-det/atom route and then try to recover the missing sharing afterward.
